    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d189">

                <pb facs="0507" n="421" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v1/pg421"/>

                <head>No. 187.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"><persName corresp="#p_SET1" type="from"
                            >Sir Edward Thornton</persName> to Mr. <persName corresp="#p_HF1"
                            type="to">Fish</persName>.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-05-01"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 1, 1873</date>.
                        (Received May 2.)</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In compliance with an instruction which I
                    have received from Lord Granville, I have the honor to transmit herewith, for
                    the information of the Government of the United States, two copies of a dispatch
                        <pb facs="0508" n="422" xml:id="pg422"/> which his lordship has addressed to
                    Her Majesty’s ambassador at Constantinople, on the report of the international
                    commission which was lately convened at Constantinople, on the subject of
                    judicial reform in Egypt, and which shows the conclusion at which Her Majesty’s
                    government has arrived.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><persName corresp="#p_SET1">EDW’D THORNTON</persName>.</signed>
                </closer>




